│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ lexicographic_polish                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the final single-minimum polishing rule with a strict lexicographic lower-tail    │
│                          │ comparator. The raw minimum remains the dominant objective: candidates are first filtered │
│                          │ to those attaining the best feasible minimum area (up to a tiny numerical tolerance).     │
│                          │ Within that plateau, the sum of the smallest eight triangle areas and then the            │
│                          │ second-smallest area are used as deterministic tie-breakers.                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is useful for Heilbronn configurations because many active or nearly active          │
│                          │ triangles create nonsmooth max-min plateaus. Pure `argmax(candidate_min)` repeatedly      │
│                          │ selects the unchanged point or an arbitrary first tied direction, whereas lower-tail      │
│                          │ shaping redistributes pressure among near-active constraints without sacrificing the      │
│                          │ achieved bottleneck. The phased 48-direction stencil also avoids repeatedly sampling the  │
│                          │ same compass directions on successive rounds.                                             │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0343                                                                                   │
